PASS  stays off in service: V1mA min 423 V vs peak 357.8 V: x1.18; 300 VAC vs 253 V: x1.19
PASS  clamp under the triac and the opto-triac: clamp 775 V at 10 A vs triac VDRM 800 V: 25 V under (3.1 %; VDSM 900 V for 10 ms, 125 V under) and vs opto-triac VDRM 800 V: 25 V under (a thin margin at the sheet's 10 A point, comfortable at the 3 to 8 A of the table below)
   the wall varistor is fed from the main board's entry clamp (14D471K) through the element, R_el 47.0 to 52.7 ohm; it never faces the generator's own source impedance, so the earlier 1200 A / 1.44 ohm arithmetic is moot
   surge table (line to neutral, 2 ohm combination-wave source [unsourced: IEC 61000-4-5 not read]): inputs marked [unsourced] are engineering estimates, not read from a document
   1 kV                         entry varistor current (1000 - 720)/2 = 140 A; wall varistor current (850 - 700)/47 = 3.2 A; triac sees at most 775 V (sheet point 775 V at 10 A); entry varistor voltage ~720 V [unsourced], entry clamp ~850 V [unsourced, 14D curve not read], wall varistor ~700 V at this current [unsourced]
PASS  wall varistor current in the surge, 1 kV: 3.2 A, far under the 1200 A single-pulse rating (x376)
   2.5 kV                       entry varistor current (2500 - 1100)/2 = 700 A; wall varistor current (1100 - 750)/47 = 7.4 A; triac sees at most 775 V (sheet point 775 V at 10 A); entry clamp ~1000 to 1100 V [unsourced], wall varistor ~750 V [unsourced]
PASS  wall varistor current in the surge, 2.5 kV: 7.4 A, far under the 1200 A single-pulse rating (x161)
   2.5 kV, entry clamp 1300 V   entry varistor current (2500 - 1300)/2 = 600 A; wall varistor current (1300 - 750)/47 = 11.7 A; triac sees about 780 V (just past the 10 A point); entry clamp as bad as 1300 V [unsourced], wall varistor ~750 V [unsourced]
PASS  wall varistor current in the surge, 2.5 kV, entry clamp 1300 V: 11.7 A, far under the 1200 A single-pulse rating (x103)
PASS  wall varistor surge energy: 775 V x 10 A x 50 us = 0.39 J against the 29 J rating (10/1000 us) (x75); an estimate, the pulse shape is not the rated one
   no varistor, 800 V triac: the triac would see the entry clamp, 850 to 1100 V [unsourced], over its VDSM 900 V at 2.5 kV: it breaks over. The wall varistor is kept
   opto-triac at the clamp voltage: it blocks 800 V (VDRM) and the clamp is at most 775 V, so it is not commanded to fire; a turn-on by dv/dt is not excluded (below), so the 775 V / 396 ohm = 1.96 A through the gate network is a rare case that can arise (amended 2026-11-18, finding 4.2); if the branch passed 800 V it would break over into the triac gate, the benign way a triac turns on
   I2t of that breakover event: 2 A^2 x 2 us [turn-on time unsourced] = 8.0e-06 A2s against the opto-triac's one cycle 1 A at 60 Hz, half-cycle I2t = 1 A^2 x 8.33 ms / 2 = 4.2e-03 A2s (x521): a comparison by I2t is an inference, not a maker figure
   varistor failed partly (fed through the element): worst dissipation at matched resistance, 253 V rms: 340.5 W in the varistor and as much again in the element (681 W together) at 2.69 A rms, which no fuse clears; the stuck-on current threshold has to catch 2.7 A (the open item: a threshold of 2.0 A or less)
PASS  partial-failure current above the carried threshold: 2.69 A against the firmware threshold the record asks (at most 2.0 A, x1.35)
   opto-triac static dv/dt: 600 V/us min, 1500 V/us typ (onsemi p.4). A front reaching 775 V in 1.2 us [unsourced estimate] is about 646 V/us: x0.93 against the minimum, x2.3 under the typical figure; a false trigger is therefore not excluded on the guaranteed figure
   if the opto-triac turns on by dv/dt (the sheet states no pulse withstand and no behaviour beyond the rating, so what follows is the ordinary thyristor mechanism, an inference): it fires the triac through the gate network, the safe way for a triac; gate current at most 775 V / 396 ohm = 1.96 A peak until the triac conducts and its main voltage collapses (the time is unsourced, microseconds; ITSM 1 A is a 60 Hz single-cycle rating, so no sheet figure covers a pulse that short)
   consequence: the triac conducts until the current next passes zero, at most one half cycle of the element: 1362 W rms x 10 ms = 13.6 J at the 47.0 ohm steam element (253 V rms), 7.6 A peak 5.4 A rms [arithmetic from the element's resistance, not a maker figure]; the opto-triac's LED is off so the next half cycle is not fired: a nuisance conduction of at most one half cycle, no hazard

== (e) triac current share, ST DS2114 Rev 11 Fig. 2 (p.4, dissipation against current) and Fig. 3 (p.4, IT(RMS) against case temperature, BTA curve)
   best-case case temperature 106.2 degC (116.4 degC less 4.84 W x 2.1 K/W). Reading 1, a straight chord from 16 A at Tc 86 degC to 0 A at 125 degC (the reviewer's reading): 7.7 A; the 0.5 current share is 3.85 A
   Reading 2, the plot is concave, not straight, so the chord understates it: the dissipation that keeps Tj at 125 degC at that case temperature is 8.94 W (18.8 K / 2.1 K/W); solving P = VTO x 0.9 I + RD I^2 (0.9 = mean of |sine|, a derivation that reproduces the sheet's 4.84 W at 5.38 A and 18.6 W at 16 A) gives 9.0 A, the 0.5 current share 4.51 A; read directly off the curve at that case temperature about 8 to 9 A [reading]
NOTE  triac current share at the best-case case temperature: the 0.5 share is 3.85 A (chord) to 4.51 A (model) against the steam element's 5.38 A: SHORT under either reading; the 8 A of row (e) is the rating at Tc 86 degC and is not the share at this case temperature. Owner decision D11 (the provisional wall cut) was accepted as preliminary by the owner on 2026-10-28; on 2026-11-19 he accepted finding 2.7 in his words as owner-sign-off.md W-5c (the 45 degC best case here is superseded history)

